WEBVTT

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.530
Sometimes, knitters make things
harder than they need to be.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.730
Here are two fast and easy
ways to increase stitches.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.370
Just pull a new needle into work.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.880
First,
if you pull a new needle on the carriage

00:00:15.946 --> 00:00:21.600
side of the knitting,
you'll form a loop on the edge.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.290
This is great if you
want a decorative edge.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.120
I've even seen seams joined this way.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.770
This technique is also used
to create a cast-on rag.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:44.920
The loops make rehanging the rag
quick and easy for fast cast-ons.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.960
To eliminate the loops,
just pull a new needle into work

00:00:49.026 --> 00:00:52.210
on the opposite side of the carriage.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.640
The edge is neat and clean,
and you'll speed through any

00:00:56.706 --> 00:01:00.320
shaping that has increases.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.210
But neatly seaming this
edge can be problematic.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:11.080
You might want to consider a different
technique, one that creates a plain

00:01:11.146 --> 00:01:15.520
stitch along the edge for seaming.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.650
The Knit It Now tutorial,
Basic Increasing and Decreasing,

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.240
shows you some more increasing options.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.970
Thanks for watching.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.970
Now repeat after me.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:35.850
Keep it simple,
and your knitting productivity will soar.

